"post_id":"Me54jJSOQdQflHQ2SMigw","description":"The Boys have made the first drop far right and have turned left for second drop.","caption":null,"poi_name":null,"subject":"Boy Scout training run","author":"Photo by Roger Nott","poi_id":"102796","photo_date":"2021-10-10T19:58:16.000Z","updated_at":"2021-10-11T19:58:16.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/X61996gVqsf4Y14IJbEaQ","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/X61996gVqsf4Y14IJbEaQ"}},{"id":"lJM0DoBHgNwNBtMhhPsur","ext":"jpg","post_id":"3VRwWyNDok0m24uQktT0Q","description":null,"caption":"Waiting to go!","poi_name":null,"subject":"Lindsey Ford Put In - AKA 6 mile Put In","author":"TerriAbbott49","poi_id":"106497","photo_date":"2021-09-04T19:29:56.000Z","updated_at":"2021-10-11T19:29:57.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/lJM0DoBHgNwNBtMhhPsur","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/lJM0DoBHgNwNBtMhhPsur"}},{"id":"DxQgLhpaAG7ogwd4t1RfX","ext":"jpg","post_id":"Q62mlNi4kOaRGVJXw6him","description":null,"caption":"Amicalola Creek @Upper","poi_name":null,"subject":null,"author":"TerriAbbott49","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":"2021-10-11T19:31:09.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/DxQgLhpaAG7ogwd4t1RfX","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/DxQgLhpaAG7ogwd4t1RfX"}},{"id":"OUoMrLTXAJGrEMnaL8zx2","ext":"jpg","post_id":"1pSTwC9V9K97bcANjABP8","description":"Just a few yards below the Lindsey Ford put in.","caption":null,"poi_name":null,"subject":"Flow at the Put in","author":"TerriAbbott49","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2021-09-04T19:36:58.000Z","updated_at":"2021-10-11T19:36:58.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/OUoMrLTXAJGrEMnaL8zx2","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/OUoMrLTXAJGrEMnaL8zx2"}},{"id":"E1AUPgAeYsjOUm7JGKgHA","ext":"jpg","post_id":"OAsJam5quWWzBEUJP8TQU","description":null,"caption":"Amicalola Creek @Upper","poi_name":null,"subject":null,"author":"TerriAbbott49","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":"2021-10-11T20:16:33.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/E1AUPgAeYsjOUm7JGKgHA","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/E1AUPgAeYsjOUm7JGKgHA"}},{"id":"lqmO8YdFd8K9pPoeafCcN","ext":"jpg","post_id":"0elBE7RftCttCsL8KDcjb","description":null,"caption":"Amicalola Creek @Upper","poi_name":null,"subject":null,"author":"TerriAbbott49","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":"2021-10-11T20:17:02.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/lqmO8YdFd8K9pPoeafCcN","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/lqmO8YdFd8K9pPoeafCcN"}},{"id":"cN8Myh7NtPZuSWDKKT9bf","ext":"jpg","post_id":"joawavPEs4nDzPBlUccRU","description":null,"caption":"Amicalola Creek @Upper","poi_name":null,"subject":null,"author":"TerriAbbott49","poi_id":null,"photo_date":null,"updated_at":"2021-10-11T20:17:35.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/cN8Myh7NtPZuSWDKKT9bf","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/cN8Myh7NtPZuSWDKKT9bf"}},{"id":"JUhxc2qD09QO2FynEu2Ji","ext":"jpg","post_id":"A5kPxMiUVEkBmtOJFY7hm","description":null,"caption":null,"poi_name":null,"subject":"Boy Scouts running Triple Drop","author":"Photo by Roger Nott","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2021-10-10T20:19:34.000Z","updated_at":"2021-10-11T20:19:35.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/JUhxc2qD09QO2FynEu2Ji","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/JUhxc2qD09QO2FynEu2Ji"}},{"id":"kGac5g4lmD3KatM1PJiK9","ext":"jpg","post_id":"E5MvBGw0eOcfPxlUnsgyH","description":null,"caption":"Let me see how this new boat works here.","poi_name":null,"subject":"Terri Abbott","author":"Photo by Roger Nott","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2021-10-10T20:25:46.000Z","updated_at":"2021-10-11T20:25:46.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/kGac5g4lmD3KatM1PJiK9","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/kGac5g4lmD3KatM1PJiK9"}},{"id":"jeJBNtmn5n7vdFxT7TmcB","ext":"jpg","post_id":"aG4090sGXgrgyuRd3iCLJ","description":"","caption":"Ben and Seth Scott","poi_name":null,"subject":"","author":"Seth Scott","poi_id":null,"photo_date":"2024-01-13T20:47:32.000Z","updated_at":"2024-01-15T20:47:32.000Z","url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/jeJBNtmn5n7vdFxT7TmcB","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/jeJBNtmn5n7vdFxT7TmcB"}}],"detail":{"id":"461","permitUrl":"","permitInfo":"","description":"The Upper Amicalola is a II/III+ run with scenery second to none. This section starts only a couple miles down stream from the famous Amicalola falls which is the tallest waterfall east of the Mississippi. It has often been referred to as a mini version of Section 3. The river has been run at levels as low as 0.4 on the GA53 gauge( would highly suggest against this unless using an ex gf/bfs gear) but a realistic minimum for the entire run is 1.1. At this level, all the rapids are runnable with a minimum amount of plastic loss from your boat but it would be a long day if you were to do the whole section. (At lower levels it is better to put in at the second or third putin.) At levels of 1.2-1.5, the river becomes a very good seasoned beginner/intermediate run with enough technical moves, eddy hopping, and surfing to keep things interesting. In my opinion it puts the Cartacay to shame for a begginer/ learning river( At lower levels\\*\\*).From 1.6-1.9 the river starts to pad out and speed up with new lines opening up. From 1.9-2.5 the river bumps up to a solid III with a significant increase in the pushiness of the drops and the amount of waves and holes on the river. At 2.5-3.5, the river becomes extremely fast and develops some huge river wide holes and standing waves up to about six feet high and becomes akin to paddling in a flood. Above 3.5 feet you realy need a bomb proof roll because if you flip and swim your gear will be gone. Also the abilitly to read water well, avoid features such as but not limited to keeper hydrolics and trees are needed. At the higher levels this becomes an excellent play boat run just make sure to watch for wood and holes.\n\n**Wood**\n\nAt levels above 2.0 feet, this section of the river moves a huge amount of wood ranging from cut firewood to 35 ft. trees. Extreme care should still be taken when boating this section at higher water levels.\n\n**Put-ins**\n\nThere are three put-ins for this section of river.\n\n**Put-in 1** Goshen Church Rd/Afton Rd (9.5 river miles total)\n\nFrom the GA53 Bridge-Go east towards Dawsonville. Take a left at the junction of GA53 and GA183. Go approximately 7.2 miles and make a left on Afton Rd. If you reach the intersection of GA183 and GA136, you have gone too far. Go 1.6 miles and put in at the bridge. The area surrounding this put-in is private land. No problems have been encountered here, but please be respectful. The river between this put-in and put-in 2 contains mostly moving water, several downed trees and a fun class two. A little after 1.5 river miles you will see the last house on river right, you will now be entering Dawson Forest.\n\n**Put-in 2** Lindsey Ford/ Six Mile / DNR Campground (7.7 river miles total)\n\nFrom the GA53 bridge-Go west 1 mile and make a right on Amicalola Church Rd. Follow the road until the pavement runs out and then continue approximately 1.5 miles. The road will begin to climb a hill and bear around to the left. There will be a brown forestry service sign on the right, next to the campground road.  This is a good putin and gets you to the first set of rapids a little faster. Now requires you to have a pass to park here. I would suggest parking on the road( not much of a shoulder) or parking in the over flow parking( where I usually park). Or you could always buy a pass ( [http://www.georgiawildlife.com/Georgia-Outdoor-Recreational-Pass](http://www.georgiawildlife.com/Georgia-Outdoor-Recreational-Pass)) If you are going this route, pass is under 10 dollars for 3 day pass but you need to do this at home because it requires a printer.\n\n**Put-in 3** Steel Bridge Road/ (Devils Elbow) (2.3 river miles total)\n\nFrom the GA53 bridge-Go west 1 mile and make a right on Amicalola Church Rd. Go approximately 3 miles and make a right on Steel Bridge Rd. Best put-in access is on the right side of the bridge. This is a excellent option if you live close and want to have a short river day. My girlfriend and I will often do this short version because there are three fun rapids with great eddy practice and you can knock it out in about a hour. This putin also requies a pass. To avoid this fee, drive over stealbridge past the user fee area sign and park on right shoulder. Again if you would like to get a GORP pass then you will have to plan ahead a bit and print one out before you leave your house. [http://www.georgiawildlife.com/Georgia-Outdoor-Recreational-Pass](http://www.georgiawildlife.com/Georgia-Outdoor-Recreational-Pass)\n\n**Local Hazards**\n\nDuring warmer weather, this section of the Amicalola is typically frequented by an armada of sit-on-tops, tubers, and Wal-Mart rafters with no regard to water level. The vast majority of these individuals are ill equipped or prepared to deal with potential problems that may occur at higher water levels.\n\n**Multi-use Area**\n\n95%+ of the navigable portion of the Amicalola lies within the Dawson Forest Wildlife Management Area. This is a multi-recreational 25,000-acre tract of land administered and managed by the Georgia Department of Natural Resources. Depending on the time of year, the forest is utilized by a wide variety of individuals including: boaters, fishermen, hunters, hikers, equestrians, and DNR personnel engaging in forestry management (controlled burns and logging operations). Please remember to be respectful of these individuals right to access and use this area.","averageGradient":"20","maxGradient":"25","length":"10.7","correlations":[{"reachID":"461","isPrimary":true,"correlationDetails":null,"migrationErrorExplanation":"Previous Flowbands: \nHigh Runnable (R6-R9): 2.6 - 3.5 cfs Adjusted Difficulty: IItoIIIplus Comment: \nMedium Runnable (R4-R6): 1.6 - 2.5 cfs Adjusted Difficulty: IItoIII Comment: \nToo High to Boat (H0-H9): 3.5 - 6 cfs Adjusted Difficulty: IIIplusstandoutIV Comment: \nLow Runnable Range (R0-R5): 1 - 1.5 cfs Adjusted Difficulty: ItoIIstandoutIII Comment: \n\nMigration Errors: \nThe boundries of the ranges do not match","gaugeInfo":{"name":"AMICALOLA CREEK NEAR DAWSONVILLE, GA.","gaugeSource":"USGS","gaugeSourceIdentifier":"02390000","gaugeFetchStatus":"ACTIVE","latestFlowReading":{"value":"97","dateTime":"2026-05-12T02:15:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"latestStageReading":{"value":"0.6","dateTime":"2026-05-12T02:15:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"location":{"latitude":"34.42627778","longitude":"-84.2125278"},"externalSourceLinks":{"sourceLink":{"url":"https://water.noaa.gov/gauges/02390000","text":"NOAA"},"secondaryLink":{"url":"https://waterdata.usgs.gov/monitoring-location/02390000/#parameterCode=00060&period=P7D","text":"US Geological Survey"}}},"comment":null,"status":{"status":null,"latestReading":{"value":"97","dateTime":"2026-05-12T02:15:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"metric":"cfs","adjustedDifficulty":null}}],"pointOfInterestIDs":["106497","1156","1157","116494"],"geometry":{"type":"LineString","coordinates":[[-84.247455,34.499744],[-84.247416,34.499707],[-84.247173,34.499522],[-84.246975,34.499356],[-84.246747,34.499226],[-84.246556,34.499117],[-84.246285,34.498972],[-84.246053,34.498858],[-84.245821,34.498767],[-84.245536,34.498644],[-84.245127,34.498491],[-84.244768,34.498401],[-84.244427,34.498209],[-84.244146,34.498087],[-84.243996,34